rota lap 4615, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; the derived row was 0 and today's logs had already read it twice while rows 1 and 2 stood at zero, so I advanced by hand. Fire's own instruction is root rather than symptom, and the threshold page asks a lap to look straight at what most laps would route around.
think opened the cold roster detached and held still while it ran, so every reading below was taken read-only against a tree that did not move. Graded all 47 docs-geode pages first: one below B, docs-geode/edu/yonder/company/seva-center-501c3-plan.md at C 74, and its drag is reach 30 rather than anything wrong. Every page reading under 100 for reach sits in edu/yonder/ except the generated libraries index, so the shipping shelf proper is healthy and the deferred room is where the prose thins. That is honest rather than a defect, so I let the C stand and looked for the fire question instead.
think the shelf front door argues at length that a typed count drifts -- three of its sentences said ten rooms while its own table listed twelve, for sixteen days -- and its repair moved the tally into the table. So I asked which pages on my shelf still type one. Five do, and one was already wrong.
obs docs-geode/etc/README.md is the room held open for a genre that does not exist yet. It named the eleven rooms standing beside it and listed all eleven by name. Twelve stand: docs-geode/lessons/ landed 20260910 in e40799aba. The one page whose whole job is to notice a new genre was the page that missed one, and its closing sentence -- the day a twelfth genre arrives, this shelf is already here to take it -- had come true a day before anybody read it.
obs my own 7c4378380 opened that page yesterday for its Style line and read past the count. Recording that because it is the ordinary way this fault survives a sweep: a lap opens a page for one reading and the other claims on it go unread.
think the root rather than the mark. tools/cr/crushed_index_witness.rish exists for exactly this fault -- its header records three typed counts wrong at once on 20260906, the shelf front door among them -- and the reading it built is MEMBERSHIP: every member of a declared room has a row on that room's index. That is why the front door's lessons row was there the day the room landed. A count or a list a page types in PROSE is read by nothing, on any page, including the four that guard declares. etc/README.md declares none at all, so it stood outside even that.
obs the sharpest instance is docs-geode/press/README.md, which types Seven pieces stand in its seventh line and the breakdown in the same paragraph that announces such counts were moved out of prose and into the table. Two free counts inside the sentence declaring the repair. Both true today, and held by nothing.
think the repair had two shapes and I took the second. Deleting the list dodges the guard and costs the reader the thing the page was for. Keeping the list and making it checkable keeps both. So the neighbors became a declared key on one line -- **Neighbors:** with a Markdown link to the parent room and the names in backticks -- the way a page already declares **Style:**, **Room:** and **Front door:**. Opting in by writing the key is the whole population filter, so no reading here guesses which sentence meant to be a list.
think named the key on the Comlink tendency, which asks three things of a new name -- clear, fun, and SAFE, where safe means it collides with nothing seated. Enumerates failed the first test, being the abstract coinage where a plain word was available. Rooms beside failed the third, by grep: two_rooms_doorway_scan_one.sh reads a page's door with \*\*Room[^:]*:\*\*, which Rooms beside matches. Nothing would have broken on this page, since its Status line already carries the room token and that scan greps both lines together -- yet a page whose Status named no room, listing a neighbor literally called vision or mixed, would have read as a room declaration it never made. Neighbors collides with nothing and says the same thing. The safety test is one grep and it earned its place before a byte landed.
obs tools/fixtures/r/room_enumeration_scan.sh resolves the key's link against the page's own directory and walls five readings at zero -- missing, phantom, no_target, absent, unreadable. The page's own room is read past, since the seated sentence is beside this one; the date, archive and yonder shelves under the parent are read past as testimony; and a name rule of ^[a-z][a-z0-9-]*$ is what keeps the link's own backticked ../ out of the claimed set.
obs a vacuum refuses rather than reading green. An empty population printed four zeros and was indistinguishable from a tree whose every list is true, which is the confident wrong zero the tree already books; it answers no_enumerations and exits 1 instead.
think two whitespace exposures found by reading rather than by failing, both the same class ascii_document_scan.sh books in its own header -- a path splits on a space. The disk-side room set was word-split, so a directory carrying a space would have arrived as two rooms the list never names, which is a false refusal and the costliest kind. And the declaring-page list was word-split too. The first is filtered to the comparable set and anything outside it is counted under unreadable and named; the second is read through a redirect rather than a pipe, which also keeps the counts out of a subshell.
obs the pre-repair reading is proven from the failing side rather than argued: the same pen, with the disk-side name rule removed, calls a healthy shelf drifted by exactly two.
obs replayed against the fault as it actually stood. A pen mirroring the real shelf's twelve room names, with lessons struck from the key, answers missing 'lessons', verdict=enumeration_drift, exit 1 -- naming the room, the page and the cause. The repaired page reads green against the same mirror.
obs REDS FIRST, MID-LAP -- three of them, all mine, every one caught by a guard in the hot pass rather than by me. tool_letter_room read misfiled=3 against a ceiling of 0: I filed the guard under tools/c/ thinking of the crushed-index family it answers, and the letter-room law files a tool by its OWN first sprig letter, which is r. The family a guard belongs to and the room its name computes are two different questions, and only the second is the law. Moved to tools/r/ and tools/fixtures/r/ with all six inbound references repointed in the same breath; ceiling_held=yes.
obs and fold_shelf_link_repoint read the elder block I had just shelved. A card block carries links written at the card's depth, and the shelf sits one directory deeper, so archive/... and ../active-designing/... both lost a level on the move. The guard computes the repair rather than naming it; tools/f/fold_shelf_link_repoint.sh --apply made both, fold_depth_lost 2 to 0. This is the shape the guard's own header calls the fourth firing of one fault, and shelving a block is how a ship meets it.
obs a THIRD red, same shape as the first two and caught the same way. shell_dialect_touch read staged_gated_sites=6 against zero: my control edited its pen with sed -i, which is GNU-only -- BSD sed -i requires a backup suffix and reads the next word as one, so the two spellings have no overlap and this tree writes neither. tools/fixtures/s/shell_portable.sh already owns sed_inplace, which writes a temporary and copies back through the original inode, preserving the mode the repository tracks. Six sites moved; control still 39 of 39.
think all three reds are one lesson wearing three coats: a new file has to satisfy laws that have nothing to do with what it measures -- which letter room its NAME computes, what depth its links are written at, and which shell dialect its idioms belong to. None of the three is discoverable from the subject I was working on, and all three were caught by guards rather than by me. That is the roster doing exactly what it is for, and it is the argument for running the hot pass before the commit rather than after.
